+its elements' array; a static of the element type is one element of it, a primitive one being
+emitted boxed already), so it aliases the static as the pointer does, and the offset names the
+first element at the element's stride. A `&str` into a static (what `str::from_utf8(&BYTES)`
+makes) is the text of those bytes, read from the static's initializer, and is refused when the
+static can be written (`static mut`, interior mutability). The binding is named, never read, so
+emitting the pointer does not depend on initialisation order. A global reference in JIR carries
+no length, so the encoder declines a wide pointer to a static and the item takes the baseline
+lane; in a body that needs semantic cleanup lowering (the release profiles) that decline is a
+compile-time refusal, which is how a `const C: &[T] = &STATIC` used in such a body fails.
+
+**Not decoded, refused at compile time.** A `&dyn Trait` in constant data — plain, or as an
+`Option<&dyn Trait>` that holds `Some` — is refused ("constants of type `dyn ..` cannot be read
+out of an allocation"); an `Option<&dyn Trait>` holding `None` compiles and reads back as `None`.
+So are a pointer one past the end of its allocation, which names no element, and a thin pointer
+into the middle of a `static`.
